                "text": "**Analyzing Line Intersection**\n\nOkay, I'm diving into this line intersection problem. I've mapped out Example 1 and noticed a color switch at the intersection. Vertical line of color 1 and horizontal of color 5 intersect. The initial input at the intersection (8, 2) is color 1, but the output becomes color 5. Why the color change? I am wondering if this is related to a weaving pattern or some sort of overlay mechanism.\n\n\n**Investigating Color Swaps**\n\nI'm now focusing on the color swaps themselves. Example 2 is particularly interesting: horizontal lines can have their colors swapped at an intersection, where their inputs are inverted. Example 3 has only one value change, with a diagonal color seemingly \"overriding\" a vertical one. Perhaps this \"override\" is what's crucial? This reminds me of the idea of \"over/under\" from earlier thoughts.\n\n\n**Confirming Color Toggle**\n\nIt seems I've cracked the code: the key is a simple inversion at the intersection. The system seemingly toggles which color \"sits on top\". This explains why the color swaps occur, as demonstrated in all the examples I've reviewed. I'm now questioning why this inversion happens and its implications. Perhaps the weave/toggle is a way to determine how the \"over\" and \"under\" are determined?\n\n\n**Simplifying Intersection Rules**\n\nI think I've overcomplicated things! It appears I may have found the core rule: Every intersection simply swaps the colors. Initial data suggests that at an intersection, the output is always the other line's color. Example 1 confirms: Input color 1 (vertical) became color 5 (horizontal). Example 2 and 3 continue to support this rule.\n\n\n**Refining Line Crossing Order**\n\nNow, I'm analyzing the implications of this color swap. I've re-examined the examples, focusing on more than just the color changes at the intersections. I've confirmed that the color swap seems to be tied to a change in the \"crossing order\" of the lines.